,"records":"Roanoke has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Roanoke's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 53\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>53\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 59\u00b0F, and the record is 78\u00b0F (1945, 1958). The low of <strong>39\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Roanoke has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Roanoke's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Roanoke has set <strong class=\"sig\">20 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 86 that chance alone would give, and <strong>70 record highs</strong>, close to the 86 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (89). Roanoke's all-time high is 105\u00b0F, on August 5, 1930.","t_chg":"Roanoke's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 111 complete years: Roanoke's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.28\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.21 to +0.35), about +3.2\u00b0F since 1912. Roanoke's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.13 to +0.06) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.95\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.68 to +1.22) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.61\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.22 to +1.01). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; May fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Roanoke's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.20 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 42.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 2020 (62.65 in); the driest, 2001 (24.94 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 13 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}